# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  What belongs in this repository is Forge itself: the Java modules, the
#  dashboard, the wishlist, the scripts and the docs. Everything below is
#  either generated, downloaded, or specific to one machine.
#
#  Root-level entries are anchored with a leading slash on purpose. An
#  unanchored rule matches at every depth, and several of these names also
#  exist in the source tree -- "index.html" alone would have excluded
#  forge/forge-app/src/main/resources/ui/index.html, which is the dashboard.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Forge's working data --------------------------------------------------
# Every project Forge has ever built. Measured 2026-10-17: 4.1 GB across
# 191,774 files, including seven git repositories of its own, their
# node_modules and their build output, plus agent transcripts that routinely
# reach hundreds of megabytes each.
/runs/

# --- Build output ----------------------------------------------------------
target/
/forge/last-good/

# --- Dependencies ----------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/

# --- Test output -----------------------------------------------------------
/ui-tests/test-results/
/ui-tests/playwright-report/
/ui-tests/.fixture/

# --- Your own input, not Forge's code --------------------------------------
# The specs are project ideas, which belong to whoever wrote them rather than
# in a public repository. Forge reads this directory; it is not part of it.
#
# The template is the exception, and is excluded from the exclusion: without it
# a clone has no valid spec to copy, and SpecLoader refuses an incomplete one.
# Ignoring the contents rather than the directory is what lets git see it.
/wishlist/*
!/wishlist/TEMPLATE.md.txt

# --- State Forge writes for itself -----------------------------------------
# All of this is rebuilt on first use. Committing it would push one machine's
# queue state, quota windows and subscription shape onto anyone who clones --
# and models.json in particular is discovered per-account, so a stale copy is
# worse than none.
/logs/
/.scratch/
/spawn/
/children.pids
/dashboard.json
/quota.json
/usage.json
/vendors.json
/models.json
# This machine's switch for publishing to GitHub. Per account, like models.json: a clone
# must start with it off, never inherit someone else's "on".
/publishing.json
/index.html
/idea-generator.json

# --- Editors and OS --------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
